{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 conflates the large service's redundancy with a counterfactual assumption that the heterogeneity budget is actually spent effectively, but its own countercondition admits that if all cells share the same flawed configuration management system, the redundancy is merely apparent. The problem stipulates the large service has 'strictly more total internal redundancy,' and SRC_001 never reconciles how 'strictly more redundancy' is meaningful when the mechanisms providing that redundancy (coordinated deployment, config management) are themselves the correlated failure vector. It smuggles in extra heterogeneity capacity not guaranteed by 'more redundancy,' so its defense of the large service rests on capabilities orthogonal to the stated comparison.","target":"138188a9e37adfd22e19cadadfa2b146a7da9f1a961e75048ee7a17240bfe46d"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 reduces resilience to dependency-graph coupling and conditional-independence testing, but it never argues for or against the claim as posed; it declares the comparison 'underspecified' and reframes it around coupling structure. The problem asks for an argument for or against a specific claim about decentralization vs. one large service with strictly more internal redundancy. By substituting a different covering principle (graph coupling) and declining to take a position on the original claim, it evades the argumentative burden. A graph could have strictly more internal redundancy yet lower coupling, or vice versa; SRC_002 never shows which dominates for the stated comparison.","target":"26e8427e560828a86799f931160a3f2176f42bf46091f467626241c4c91b01ed"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's own countercondition undercuts its absolute claim: if small services are truly minimal (e.g., static sites with no shared runtime dependencies), correlated failures are less likely. But the problem explicitly asks to compare decentralized networks against 'one large service with strictly more total internal redundancy.' A minimal-footprint decentralized network can have fewer correlated failure paths than a large service whose extra redundancy shares common infrastructure layers. SRC_003 asserts decentralized networks are 'systematically more vulnerable' yet cannot explain why a large service with strictly more redundancy is immune to the same shared-dependency critique it levels at small services; a large service also depends on common libraries, cloud providers, and runtime infrastructure. The asymmetry SRC_003 claims is not established.","target":"5b1355c87ee7ce260726e375ca9a460802500b7611ccbd45f93d35fb627a8f17"}]}